// ADR 0051 / ADR 0016, register D4: a generic USER fn's type argument is seeded from the
// EXPECTED RETURN TYPE before any argument is inferred, so the ARGUMENT widens and the
// result does not.
//
// `let s: secret i64 = idg(7)` with `fn idg<T>(x: T) -> T`: the oracle unifies the
// signature's return `T` against the expectation `secret i64` FIRST, monomorphises at
// `secret i64`, and widens the public literal `7` at the call — emitting no wrapper
// around the result at all. `scg` had no expectation to seed from (`dump_generic_call`
// was the one call sub-path that never received `exp`), so it monomorphised at `i64` and
// wrapped OUTSIDE: a different mangled symbol AND a different instruction sequence.
//
// ⚠ THE SEED IS GATED ON `ntp > 0` AND AN EXPECTATION, NOTHING ELSE. The oracle's own
// comment says it seeds only when "the signature's return type is exactly a TypeParam",
// and its code does no such check. Its sibling fixture
// `c17_generic_return_only_infer.sentinel` is the discriminator: that return type is a
// NULLABLE, not a bare type param, and it must still seed through the `?` shell. Mirror
// the code, not the comment.
//
// ⚠ WHY THIS IS A `tests/pass` FIXTURE AND NOT JUST A DIFFERENTIAL NOTE: the pre-fix
// divergence was not cosmetic. For a `?bool` expectation the same inversion emitted
// `store { i1, i1 } %v0` with `%v0` an `i1`, which `llvm-as` rejects.
//
// 7 + 35 = 42.
fn idg<T>(x: T) -> T {
    x
}

fn main() -> i64 {
    let s: secret i64 = idg(7);
    declassify(s) + 35
}
